Beryllium: least Capsicum rights on descriptors a confined process creates or receives

Purpose: Phase 2 increment 3 (plan/14-RENDERER-SANDBOX.md; feature matrix
  S-REN-04, S-REN-05, S-IPC-01). The renderer lockdown
  (beryllium-capsicum-renderer.patch) cuts every descriptor that exists at
  the lockdown point to the rights its kind needs. The owner's runs of
  0.20.3.8 (7 November 2026, with security.bsd.unprivileged_proc_debug=1)
  showed what that leaves: everything a renderer creates or receives after
  the lockdown has every right. Per renderer that was a kqueue and an
  eventfd for each thread that starts a message pump after the lockdown
  (the IO thread, the compositor and others), every socket the browser
  sends for a transport to another process, and the font files the font
  service passes. Capsicum has no default rights for new descriptors, so
  each must be cut where it appears. This patch adds, on FreeBSD only:
  - base/posix/capsicum_rights.{h,cc}: the policy in one place. A kind per
    descriptor (read-only file, socket, kqueue, eventfd or pipe, shared
    memory, directory) with its rights, fcntl commands and ioctls, taken
    as the intersection with what the descriptor has (rights only ever
    shrink, so the kernel never refuses); the kind from a kinfo_file entry
    (for the lockdown sweep, outside capability mode) and from fstat for a
    process in capability mode, where kern.proc.filedesc and F_KINFO are
    refused: an empty kevent answers only on a kqueue and F_GET_SEALS only
    on shared memory, and neither needs a right. The lockdown patch now
    uses this instead of its own copy.
  - base/message_loop/message_pump_epoll.cc: each pump cuts its epoll
    descriptor (epoll-shim's kqueue on FreeBSD: kevent, event, fstat) and
    its wake eventfd at creation, in every process. The survey of 0.20.3.8
    found 6 million refusals after the lockdown because the kqueue policy
    lacked CAP_EVENT while epoll-shim's epoll_wait() polls its kqueue
    before each kevent(); the policy here includes it.
  - mojo/public/cpp/platform/socket_utils_posix.cc: a process in
    capability mode cuts every descriptor it receives over a Mojo channel
    (SocketRecvmsg serves both the ipcz transports and the broker client)
    to its kind's rights, and removes every right from one the policy has
    no kind for (a writable file, a device...), logging what it was; the
    descriptor stays, so whoever expected it gets one that can do nothing
    rather than a missing handle.
  This is defence in depth for a renderer that is not compromised, and it
  makes the kernel's view match the policy (procstat -C, the suite's
  policy test). It is not the security boundary: a compromised renderer
  can call recvmsg itself. The boundary is the browser limiting what it
  sends (S-IPC-01), the next increment, which needs to know which child a
  transport leads to.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). beryllium-capsicum-renderer.patch depends on
  this one (the helper); applies after the imported FreeBSD patches
  patch-base_BUILD.gn and patch-base_message__loop_message__pump__epoll.cc
  (stage 2).
  Sockets (7 November 2026, after the adversarial review, plan/16 BER-02):
  a socket is admitted only in the shape of a Mojo transport, an end of an
  unnamed AF_UNIX stream or seqpacket pair, not listening, bound or
  connected to no path (kinfo_file kf_sock_domain, kf_sock_type,
  kf_sa_local, kf_sa_peer at the lockdown; SO_DOMAIN, SO_TYPE,
  SO_ACCEPTCONN, getsockname, getpeername for a received one). A network
  socket or one connected to a named service keeps authority that rights
  cannot take back, so it ends the lockdown or loses every right.
  The receive window (0.20.3.10, 7 November 2026): the survey of a confined
  renderer showed two sockets with every right among limited ones. The
  lockdown runs with the IO thread already receiving Mojo messages, so a
  descriptor that arrives between the table snapshot and cap_enter() was in
  neither the sweep nor the receive path (which waited for capability
  mode). ExpectConfinement(), called by the lockdown before the snapshot,
  turns the receive-path limits on from that moment. The same limit now
  also covers base::UnixDomainSocket::RecvMsgWithFlags (the sandbox IPC
  replies that carry font files), the other recvmsg path in the renderer.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. The 0.20.3.10
  state compiled and ran (7 November 2026); the receive window and
  UnixDomainSocket changes are for build 0.20.3.11, not yet compiled.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py
